{
  "id": "COMPOUND:31124 - PROTEIN:6326",
  "verbalization": "Penitrem C is implicated in the targeting of Integrin beta-6, which is a key component of the extracellular matrix and plays a role in cell adhesion and signaling. This relationship is further supported by the fact that Integrin beta-6 is a target of multiple compounds, including (1aR,7R,7bS)-1a-methyl-7-propan-2-yl-2,3,5,6,7,7b-hexahydronaphtho[1,2-b]oxiren-4-one, [(1S,2S,4S,5S,6R,10S)-5-hydroxy-10-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxy-3,9-dioxatricyclo[4.4.0.02,4]dec-7-en-2-yl]methyl (E)-3-(3-hydroxy-4-methoxyphenyl)prop-2-enoate, and (1E,4Z,6E)-5-Hydroxy-1,7-Bis(4-Hydroxyphenyl)Hepta-1,4,6-Trien-3-One. All these compounds are known to target Phosphodiesterase 6C, suggesting a common mechanism of action involving the modulation of phosphodiesterase activity. The evidence indicates that Penitrem C may act through a pathway that involves the interaction with Integrin beta-6, which in turn influences the activity of Phosphodiesterase 6C. This suggests a potential role for Penitrem C in modulating cellular processes through the regulation of integrin-mediated signaling and subsequent effects on phosphodiesterase activity.",
